/**

@page installation <h2>Installation</h2>

@todo figure out how to use footnotes. classical html methods seem not to work, and doxygen has no keyword for them

There are two ways to obtain Peano: 
You can either download one of the archives we provide on the webpage, or you
can work directly against a repository clone (recommended).
We recommend to work against the latest release of Peano which you can get through:

<pre>
git clone -b p4 https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano.git
</pre>


in the directory where you've stored your downloaded file. 
If you work with the archive, type in

<code> <b> 
tar -xzvf myarchive.tar.gz
</b></code> 

in the directory where you've stored your downloaded file.

<h2> Prepare the configure environment</h2>

First, ensure you have the autotool packages installed on your system. They
typically are shipped in the packages <code> <b>autoconf</b></code>, <code> <b>automake</b></code> and
<code> <b>libtool</b></code>.
Run the line below in the shell to set up the configure environment. These steps should only be required once, unless you push major revisions to the
development branch.

<code> <b> libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing; </b></code>

<h2> Configuration </h2>
The steps below will guide you through configuring and building the Peano code.
An example of a shell script which summarises these steps can be found 
\ref sample_installation_script "here."


Peano  relies on the autotools to set up its build environment.
Change into the project's directory and type in: 

<code> <b>./configure --help</b></code>

The <code> <b> --help </b></code> option should give you some information about the available
variants of Peano.
A sole <code> <b>./configure</b></code>
call gives you the minimalist version of
Peano, which might be appropriate for a first test.

@remark 
I recommend that you start a first test without any additional flavours of
Peano, i.e. to work with a plain <code> <b>./configure </b></code> call. Once the tests
pass, I recommend that you add further options (IO) and/or parallelisation.
Please note that the baseline config is not sufficient for most applications
as it is merely a stripped down version of the Peano core.
ExaHyPE for example requires you to add <code> <b>--enable-exahype</b></code>,
<code> <b>--enable-loadbalancing-toolbox</b></code> and <code> <b>--enable-blockstructured</b></code>. 
But the bare/minimal version is good for first tests.

The output of <code> <b>./configure --help</b></code> should be reasonably verbose, but I 
summarise some of the most important options below:

<table> 
  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b> --with-multithreading </b> </code>
      <code> <b>  </b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Switch on multithreading. By default, we
      build without multithreading, but using, for instance, <code> <b> --with-multithreading=omp </b> </code>
      makes Peano  use the OpenMP threading model. Please consult <code> <b> --help </b> </code> for
      details. Note: To use OpenMP multithreading, you will need a compiler which
      supports OpenMP 5.0 (e.g - GCC 9.x)[fn]]Peano realises its GPGPU
     offloading via OpenMP, too. Some compilers struggle to translate these GPGPU
      instructions. In this case, please configure with the CXXFLAG
     <code> <b> -DnoGPUOffloading </b> </code> .[/fn].
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      <code> <b> --with-mpi </b> </code>
      <code> <b>  </b> </code>
    </th>

    <th>
      Enable the MPI version of Peano. You have to tell the
      build environment however which compile command to use. Please note that
      we need a C++ MPI wrapper. So <code> <b>  --with-mpi=mpcxx</b> </code> is a typical call. Currently, you also need  <code> <b> --with-multithreading </b> </code> if you want to use MPI. 
    </th>
  </tr>

  <tr>
    <th>
      \texttt{--with-toolchain}
    </th>

    <th>
      Build against a particular vendor toolchain.
      Currently, I have dedicated support for the NVIDIA NVTX SDK
      (\texttt{--with-toolchain=nvidia}) providing NVIDIA-specific logging/tracing
      as well as memory allocation calls---the latter is of use if you use
      GPUs---and the Intel toolchain (\texttt{--with-toolchain=intel}). The latter
      in particular links against the ITAC tracing API. I also have support for
      Google's Chrome browser (\texttt{--with-toolchain=chrome}) which means that
      traces are written in a way that you can display it in Chrome. Ensure that your CXXFLAGS set the right include path\footnote{It is
      \texttt{CXXFLAGS+=-I/opt/nvidia/nsight-systems/2020.3.1/target-linux-x64/nvtx/include/nvtx3/}
      on my system, e.g.}.
      This is optional.
    </th>
  </tr>


  
</table>
*/



